<TopicPage slug="subjects/philosophy/advanced/phenomenology-and-existentialism">
  <h2>Where you are, and what you will learn</h2>
  <p>
    This is an advanced Philosophy lesson. It assumes the intermediate <em>Philosophy of Mind</em>
    lesson — dualism, physicalism and the functionalist attempt to locate mental states — and,
    further back, the foundational lessons on metaphysics and epistemology, which set the questions
    of what exists and what we can know. Phenomenology reopens both questions from inside
    experience itself.
  </p>
  <p>By the end you will be able to:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>define intentionality as aboutness and explain why it is not a causal relation;</li>
    <li>state what qualia are and how Nagel's bat and Mary's room pose complementary puzzles for physicalism;</li>
    <li>run Husserl's epoché as a method — describing the correlation between act and object without settling whether the object exists;</li>
    <li>use Heidegger's being-in-the-world, das Man and authenticity, and Sartre's bad faith, as diagnostic tools;</li>
    <li>explain de Beauvoir's situated freedom and its consequence: willing my freedom requires willing the conditions for everyone's.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    The philosophy-of-mind lessons have been asking what mental states <em>are</em> — dualist substances,
    physicalist brain states, functional roles. Phenomenology attacks the question from inside: before we
    theorise about minds, we should describe <strong>what experience is actually like</strong> — how a melody
    unfolds, how a hammer feels in use, how another's gaze changes a room. That descriptive project
    (Husserl's phenomenology) and the question it forces — how a free, finite being should live inside such
    experience (the existentialists: Kierkegaard, Heidegger, Sartre, de Beauvoir) — reshaped philosophy,
    psychiatry, literature and feminism. Two technical concepts from the syllabus anchor it:
    <strong>intentionality</strong> and <strong>qualia</strong>.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the method, the immersion, the freedom, the situation" widget="equationStep" config={phenCfg} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> the four cards are a chronology, and each step tightens the
    previous one. Husserl supplies the method (describe experience as lived); Heidegger dissolves
    the detached observer the method still presupposed; Sartre draws the freedom that follows;
    de Beauvoir situates that freedom socially. Move back and forth between the cards and the
    sections below — every section is one card unpacked with its critics attached.
  </p>

  <h2>Intentionality: consciousness is always about something</h2>
  <p>
    Franz Brentano revived the scholastic term <em>intentionality</em> for the mark of the mental: every
    conscious state is <strong>directed at an object</strong> — hoping for rain, fearing the exam, loving a
    person, thinking about Vienna. There is no such thing as bare awareness; awareness is always awareness
    <em>of</em>. This is not the same as causal relation (I can fear a ghost that does not exist; the object of
    a thought can be absent, impossible, or merely imagined), and that "aboutness without contact" is exactly
    what physical descriptions of the brain struggle to capture — it is why intentionality remains a frontline
    topic in the philosophy of mind. Husserl made it the foundation of a method: attend to the act and its
    object <em>as they present themselves in experience</em>, setting aside (bracketing, the
    <strong>epoché</strong>) the question of whether the object exists out there. The point is not to deny the
    world but to describe the <strong>correlation</strong> — how a cube is necessarily given as a perspectival
    glimpse with unseen sides meant, how a melody is present as retention of the just-past and anticipation of
    the next note. Husserl's time-consciousness analyses (retention, primal impression, protention) anticipated
    by decades the psychology of temporal perception.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "intentional means deliberate — an intentional act is one you
  meant to do." That is the everyday word, and it is not Brentano's term. In this lesson's sense,
  <em>every</em> conscious state is intentional, including the accidental and the unwanted: a
  sudden itch, a fear you tried to suppress, a melody that ambushes you are all still
  <em>about</em> something. The technical claim is about directedness, not deliberateness — which
  is exactly why it applies to absent and impossible objects too: you can fear a ghost, plan for a
  future that never arrives. Directedness is the mark of the mental; deliberateness is only one of
  its many moods.</p>

  <h2>Qualia: the what-it-is-like structure</h2>
  <p>
    Where intentionality is the aboutness of experience, <strong>qualia</strong> are its felt qualities — the
    phenomenal character of seeing red, tasting coffee, feeling pain: what makes experience have a
    <em>what-it-is-like</em>. The syllabus pairs the two because they pose complementary puzzles for
    physicalism. A complete functional account of a bat's echolocation might still leave open what it is like
    <em>for the bat</em> (Nagel, 1974); Mary the colour scientist who knows every physical fact about red but
    has never seen it seems to learn something new on her first look (Jackson, 1982). Phenomenology's wager is
    that these puzzles are generated by third-person theorising that has skipped the first-person data: before
    modelling qualia as information states, describe them faithfully — their felt intensity, their
    figure-ground structure, their inescapable mineness. Critics reply that introspection is theory-laden and
    unreliable; the dialogue between phenomenological description and cognitive science is one of the live
    research programmes you met flagged in the physicalism lesson.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> phenomenology is not about "inner private mental theatre." Husserl's and
  especially Merleau-Ponty's descriptions centre the <em>world</em> as experienced — the tool ready-to-hand,
  the other person, the street at dusk. The unit of analysis is the subject-world relation, not a sealed
  internal screen.</p>
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> intentionality and qualia name the two puzzles any theory of mind must
    survive, and Husserl's method answers with disciplined first-person description. Next, the
    method turned against itself: Heidegger argues the describing subject is never detached in the
    first place.
  </p>

  <h2>Heidegger: being-in-the-world</h2>
  <p>
    Heidegger's <em>Being and Time</em> (1927) radicalised the method by attacking its residual assumption —
    that a subject first exists and then meets objects. Dasein (his name for us) is <strong>being-in-the-world</strong>:
    always already immersed in a web of tools, projects, places and other people. His most portable observation
    is about <strong>readiness-to-hand</strong>: the hammer shows up in experience primarily as transparent —
    something we use unthinkingly — and only becomes a salient "object" (present-at-hand) when it breaks. Most
    of life runs on this absorbed coping, not on detached perception, a claim embodied-cognition research has
    since taken very seriously. Structuring it all are <strong>moods</strong> — not decorations on cognition but
    disclosures of the world's significance (anxiety, uniquely, reveals the world as such, stripped of familiar
    meanings). And most of our self-understanding is borrowed from <strong>das Man</strong>, "the They": we
    speak, choose and judge as one does. <strong>Authenticity</strong> is the counter-move — owning your
    finitude (being-toward-death, which no one can do for you) and your situation, instead of drifting in
    They-self averages. However one judges the book's politics (and Heidegger's later Nazi entanglement
    indelibly stains it), its questions set the agenda.
  </p>

  <h2>Sartre: existence precedes essence — and bad faith</h2>
  <p>
    Sartre's slogan, <strong>"existence precedes essence,"</strong> inverts theology's ordering: a paperknife
    is conceived before it is made (essence first), but a human being exists first and <em>invents</em> what
    they are through choices — there is no blueprint, divine or psychological. The consequence is radical
    freedom: we are "condemned to be free," responsible even for the meanings our situation has, since
    situations constrain but never determine choice. The characteristic evasion is <strong>bad faith</strong>
    (mauvaise foi): lying to oneself that one is a thing with a fixed nature. His waiter plays at being a
    waiter — performing the role as if it were his essence rather than a role he is freely enacting; the
    frightened lover who "couldn't help it," the employee who is "just following the rules," the student who
    "has no choice" — all are attempts to hide freedom from oneself. Sartre is not naive: the Looking-Glass
    analysis of <strong>the Look</strong> (being seen turns me into an object in another's world) and his
    account of anguish show how heavily social existence bears. But the thesis stands:anguish is the
    recognition that no authority — God, instinct, therapist, data — can make the choice for you, which is why
    his existentialism is an ethics of responsibility before it is a psychology.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> freedom without a blueprint, and bad faith as the flight from it. But
    Sartre's free agent risks sounding unencumbered — the next section supplies the correction
    that freedom is always exercised from a situation, with consequences for everyone else's
    freedom too.
  </p>

  <h2>De Beauvoir: freedom, situated</h2>
  <p>
    Simone de Beauvoir's <em>The Second Sex</em> (1949) is where existentialism meets empirical situation —
    and corrects Sartre's tendency to imagine the free agent unencumbered. Her master sentence: <strong>"one
    is not born, but rather becomes, a woman."</strong> The claim is not that biological sex is nothing, but
    that the meaning of the female body — the roles, expectations, "feminine" essences — is socially
    constructed through history, so that woman is positioned as Other, the deviation from an unmarked male
    norm. Existentially this is a systematic denial of transcendence: a freedom offered a diminished menu of
    projects. Her <em>Ethics of Ambiguity</em> (1947) draws the political consequence that Sartre's early
    absolutist freedom missed: <strong>freedom is situated</strong> — my freedom is real but materially and
    socially conditioned, and (the ethical turn) it genuinely depends on the freedom of others, so willing my
    own freedom requires willing the conditions for everyone's. This move founded socialist feminism's
    theoretical core and made "becoming" — gender as enacted project, not given essence — the ancestor of
    contemporary gender theory. Existentialism and feminism changed each other here.
  </p>

  <h2>The method in practice: one description, done properly</h2>
  <p>
    The epoché sounds mystical until you run it. Take the most ordinary act — drinking coffee — and
    describe only what presents itself, bracketing physics, chemistry and the coffee industry. What shows
    up first is not "brown liquid in a cup" but a <strong>field of solicitation</strong>: the cup matters
    as to-be-drunk, its handle already pointing at your hand (Heidegger's readiness-to-hand, visible in
    miniature). The aroma is not a neutral stimulus but announces "morning," the ongoing project of
    starting the day; the warmth arrives as expectation-fulfilled — the first sip confirms what the steam
    promised, and if the coffee is cold the <em>violation</em> reveals the expectation you did not know
    you had. Time-consciousness is everywhere: each sip is retained as the just-past shaping the next
    (the third sip is "the coffee cooling"), and the cup's far side is co-given as meant, not seen — the
    perspectival structure Husserl made central. And the whole experience is already relational: coffee
    with a friend is a different phenomenon from coffee alone, and none of that difference is in the
    liquid. This is what phenomenological data look like: fine-grained, first-person, and testable
    against others' descriptions — the raw material neurophenomenology then pairs with brain dynamics,
    and the exercise any reader can perform tonight.
  </p>

  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "existentialism means anything goes — if there is no God, do whatever
  you like." Sartre's reply is explicit and often overlooked: without a pre-given essence, responsibility
  <em>increases</em> rather than evaporates — no commandment, no human nature, no therapist can carry the
  weight of the choice, so the chooser owns it entirely. And because choosing is choosing <em>for</em>
  (each act endorses a value as if for everyone, in the Kantian move Sartre borrows), bad faith and
  whim are both failures of the freedom they claim to exercise. De Beauvoir's <em>Ethics of Ambiguity</em>
  draws the same conclusion socially: the nihilist and the "serious man" who hides in ready-made values
  are evasions in opposite directions; genuine freedom — yours or anyone's — requires willing the
  conditions under which others can choose at all.</p>

  <h2>Check yourself</h2>
  <p>Try these before moving on. Feedback follows each answer.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>Recall: what exactly does the epoché bracket, and what is left over for description once it does?</li>
    <li>Application: Sartre's waiter performs his role with an enthusiasm that feels slightly off. Explain the performance as bad faith — and explain why having a job is not itself bad faith.</li>
    <li>Why does de Beauvoir insist that willing my own freedom requires willing the freedom of others?</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    <strong>Feedback:</strong> (1) The epoché brackets the question of whether the object exists
    out there — not the object as experienced. What remains is the correlation: how a cube is
    given perspectivally with unseen sides meant, how a melody is retained and anticipated. The
    aim is description, not denial of the world. (2) Bad faith is treating a role as a fixed
    essence — playing at being a waiter as if the role, not his freedom, were doing the choosing.
    The job itself is not the problem; any role can also be freely taken up and re-chosen, and
    the difference shows when the situation demands something the "essence" cannot cover. (3)
    Because freedom is situated: my projects are built from the meanings, conditions and
    opportunities a social world provides, so my freedom is materially dependent on everyone
    else's. De Beauvoir's ethical turn follows — willing my freedom honestly means willing the
    conditions under which others can choose at all.
  </p>

  <h2>Key takeaways</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Intentionality: every conscious state is about something — aboutness without contact, including absent and impossible objects.</li>
    <li>Qualia are experience's felt qualities; Nagel's bat and Mary's room are complementary puzzles for physicalism.</li>
    <li>The epoché brackets existence-questions to describe the act–object correlation precisely.</li>
    <li>Heidegger: we are absorbed, tool-using being-in-the-world; das Man supplies borrowed selves; authenticity owns finitude.</li>
    <li>Sartre: existence precedes essence, responsibility is total, and bad faith is hiding freedom from yourself.</li>
    <li>De Beauvoir: freedom is situated, and ethically entangled with everyone else's freedom.</li>
    <li>The method yields fine-grained first-person data — the raw material of neurophenomenology.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Where this goes next</h2>
  <p>
    The advanced lesson <em>Language &amp; Meaning</em> takes up the challenge this page's critics
    raised: if the "choosing self" is itself shaped by language and institutions, then meaning —
    not lived description — is the primary datum, and the linguistic turn runs straight through
    the phenomenologist's claims. The advanced lesson <em>Modal Logic</em> supplies the formal
    machinery for the possibility-talk this lesson has used loosely all along.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Merleau-Ponty closes the loop.</strong> His <em>Phenomenology of Perception</em> (1945) argues the
    body is not a vehicle the mind drives but the <strong>subject of perception itself</strong> — the
    "body-subject" that skilfully copes before it represents. His case studies (the phantom limb that persists
    as a bodily project of a pre-war world; Schneider, the brain-injured patient who can perform abstract tasks
    but not concrete ones) prefigure what cognitive science now calls embodied, enactive cognition. Varela,
    Thompson and colleagues later built <strong>neurophenomenology</strong> explicitly on it: pair
    first-person trained description with brain dynamics, treating lived experience as data, not noise.
    Dennett's counterproposal — <strong>heterophenomenology</strong>, treating subjects' reports as fiction to
    be explained rather than data to be honoured — marks the naturalist dissent, and the debate is unresolved.
  </p>
  <p>
    Three further strata for research-level reading. <strong>The genealogy of the movement</strong>: Kierkegaard
    (anguish, the single individual, the stages of life) and Nietzsche (death of God, ressentiment, self-creation)
    supply the pre-history; Camus translates the problem of meaning into the absurd; Frankl's
    <strong>logotherapy</strong> — forged in the camps, "he who has a <em>why</em> to live can bear almost any
    <em>how</em>" — is existentialism's most direct clinical descendant, alongside modern existential therapy
    and ACT's work on acceptance of finitude. <strong>The social critiques</strong>: Fanon extended de Beauvoir's
    framework to racialised embodiment ("I am overdetermined from without"); Sartre's later Marxist phase tried
    to reconcile radical freedom with class structure. <strong>The standing objections</strong>: analytic
    critics charge phenomenology with unverifiable introspectionism; Marxists with bourgeois individualism;
    structuralists (and later postmodernists) with ignoring that the "choosing self" is itself an effect of
    language and institutions — a challenge the next lessons on postmodernism take up directly.
  </p>
  <p>
    What survives every critique is the pairing this lesson began with. Intentionality and qualia name the two
    genuine novelties consciousness contributes to the world — aboutness and feel — and no complete naturalism
    can decline to explain them. The phenomenologists' demand stands as method, not mysticism: any theory of
    mind that cannot be squared with a careful description of how a melody is heard has explained something,
    but not experience.
  </p>

  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Husserl, <em>Logical Investigations</em> (1900); Heidegger, <em>Being and Time</em> (1927); Merleau-Ponty, <em>Phenomenology of Perception</em> (1945); Sartre, <em>Being and Nothingness</em> (1943); de Beauvoir, <em>The Second Sex</em> (1949) and <em>The Ethics of Ambiguity</em> (1947); Frankl, <em>Man's Search for Meaning</em> (1946); Nagel (1974); Jackson (1982). Source text: OpenStax (https://openstax.org), CC BY 4.0 — adapted for Interactive Atlas.</p>
